                <pb facs="0976" n="882" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg882"/>

                <head>No. 509.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Romero to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of Mexico</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Washington, D.
                                C.</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1887-07-17"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 17, 1887</date>. (Received July
                        18.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Bayard</hi>: Referring to our interview
                    of last Friday concerning the message sent to you by Mr. Hall, I have the
                    pleasure to inform you that I have just received a cablegram from Señor
                    Mariscal, dated at Mexico City yesterday, in reply to the one I sent him on the
                    15th instant, in compliance with your request, and he desires me to tell you
                    that the Mexican troops ordered to the ‘Guatemalan frontier were not sent there
                    to provoke a collision, but simply to protect Mexican interests.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">C. Romero.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
